<header class="page-head docs-head">
    <div>
        <h1>Razor, metacode and decompilation</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            Three ways of reading code that is not plain C# in a Roslyn
            project: Razor pages, canonical pseudocode shared by every
            language, and compiled assemblies — managed or native.
        </p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="docs-body prose">
    <h2>Razor and Blazor pages</h2>
    <p>
        <code>.cshtml</code> and <code>.razor</code> files are not Roslyn
        documents: the Razor SDK compiles them with a different toolchain.
        VisualMCP reads them <strong>from disk</strong>, with a dedicated
        scanner that maps each file to a page model — which also means the
        analysis works without a loaded solution, useful for auditing a
        repository you have not opened.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Answers</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>razor_pages</code></td><td>What exists and how it is wired: kind, <code>@@page</code> route, <code>@@model</code>, layout, sections, <code>@@inject</code>, handlers, code-behind, markup counts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>analyze_razor_pages</code></td><td>What is wrong with the markup: broken HTML, accessibility, Razor misuse, maintainability.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>find_razor_vulnerabilities</code></td><td>What is exploitable: XSS, CSRF, secrets, unsafe URLs, CSP — each finding with a CWE id.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>razor_edit</code></td><td>The write side: six verified text splices.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        The security rules know Razor: <code>@@Html.Raw</code> with an
        unencoded value, model data inside a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block
        or an inline handler, <code>javascript:</code> URLs, POST forms with
        no anti-forgery token, passwords submitted with GET, hardcoded
        credentials, <code>target="_blank"</code> without
        <code>noopener</code>. Values already passed to an encoder do not
        fire. It is a heuristic analysis — no data flow — so a clean result is
        not a security proof.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>razor_edit</code> performs one verified splice per call: set or
        remove an attribute on the <em>n</em>-th element with a tag, add a
        directive, insert, replace or delete lines. The <code>expect</code>
        argument takes the text the target lines must currently hold — if the
        file no longer matches, the edit is refused. The page is re-scanned
        before writing, and the result reports the findings the edit resolved
        and introduced.
    </p>

    <h2>Metacode</h2>
    <p>
        Metacode is one canonical pseudocode model for syntax trees from
        different languages. The same algorithm prints the same body, line
        for line, whichever frontend produced it: uppercase keywords, one
        statement per line, operator precedence preserved.
    </p>
    <pre><code>metacode_translate  path=Services/OrderService.cs
metacode_translate  path=Pages/Checkout.cshtml
metacode_compare    leftPath=OrderService.cs rightPath=OrderService.kt.mc</code></pre>
    <ul>
        <li>C#, Visual Basic, Razor, JavaScript, Java, Python, C++ and Rust are all inputs — the last three through their own toolchains, lowered into the same model.</li>
        <li>A construct the model has no node for stays visible as <code>RAW[lang]</code> with a diagnostic: incomplete translation is visible, never silent.</li>
        <li><code>metacode_compare</code> is the equivalence check: two spellings of an algorithm compared line by line, which is how you review a port or verify that a refactor kept the shape of a body.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Decompiling managed assemblies</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>decompile_type</code></td><td>One type from a <code>.dll</code> back to C#, with ILSpy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>decompile_assembly</code></td><td>A whole module, or a filtered, paged set of types; can export one file per type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>inspect_assembly</code></td><td>The public API — types, base types, interfaces, members — without running anything.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>decompile_to_metacode</code></td><td>ILSpy → C# → the metacode model, printed as pseudocode instead of C#.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>inspect_package</code>, <code>decompile_package_type</code></td><td>The same, starting from a NuGet package id and version.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Recovering native binaries</h2>
    <p>
        <code>analyze_native_binary</code> runs <strong>Ghidra headless</strong>
        on a PE/ELF/Mach-O file, exports every function's decompiled C and
        lowers it into the same model:
    </p>
    <pre><code>analyze_native_binary  binaryPath=C:\Windows\System32\notepad.exe maxFunctions=5</code></pre>
    <p>
        It is a real external run — tens of seconds, bounded by a timeout that
        kills the whole process tree. Narrow it with a function name or entry
        address, or a maximum function count. Ghidra is installed on demand
        into the per-user tools folder when no installation is found, and the
        binary never leaves the machine.
    </p>

    <h2>Decompiled code lives in the same AST</h2>
    <p>
        What gets decompiled is stored in the <strong>same tables</strong> as
        the solution's own code, under <code>assembly://</code> and
        <code>native://</code> keys, with provenance columns that say which
        file it came from and that it was decompiled. The complexity ranking,
        the metacode queries and a raw SQL query never confuse recovered code
        with source you own — so “what does this library actually do?” is
        answered with the same tools that answer it about your own code.
    </p>
</div>

